\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Frame eigenvalues on a finite orbit}
\date{2026-11-21}

\p{Let #{A} be a symmetric matrix, indexed by a finite #{G}-orbit,
with nonnegative entries in an ordered field, and assume the
Rayleigh and connectedness hypotheses of \ref{fdip-011X}. Then the largest eigenvalue is simple, it is at least the absolute
value of every eigenvalue in the ordered field, and it has an
entrywise positive eigenvector. If the orbit
has at least two points, the complement of that eigenline is
nonzero. If some power of #{A} is entrywise positive, every other
absolute value is strictly smaller. The unitary gauge of
\ref{fdip-0123} retains the reference point of each sector and the
reduced link. An eigenfunction of #{A} assigns a scalar to an
orbit point, so it is not a coordinate of that reduced link.}

\proof{
\p{The spectral conclusions are \ref{fdip-011X}, including
simplicity of the largest eigenvalue. On a space of dimension at
least two, the complement of a line is nonzero. \ref{fdip-0123}
replaces the orbit point at each vertex by the reference point of
its sector and retains the reduced link. The index set of #{A} is
the orbit, so a function of that index is not a function of the
retained link.}
}
